#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""image-use — generate images using your ChatGPT subscription, no API key.

Two backends produce the same kind of bitmap, but bill different buckets:

  * web (default): drives your already-logged-in ChatGPT browser via
    `chrome-use`, generating on the ChatGPT *conversation* surface. The real
    browser produces the Cloudflare Turnstile token a headless client can't
    forge — that, not Cloudflare's edge or the sentinel proof-of-work (both
    passable bare), is the actual wall. This does NOT consume the metered
    Codex-usage limit —
    it's the same surface as typing in the app. Needs a logged-in browser open.
    Conversations are grouped into a ChatGPT Project (default "imagegen",
    created on first use) so they don't litter the chat history.

  * codex: a headless POST to `chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex/responses` with the
    `image_generation` tool, reusing the OAuth token from `~/.codex/auth.json`
    (`codex login`). Fast and dependency-free, but every call bills the metered
    Codex-usage bucket.

Usage:
    image-use "a watercolor cat"                       # web backend (default)
    image-use "logo" -o brand/logo.png --size 1024x1024
    image-use "hero banner" -o web/hero.png --backend codex

Setup (one-time):
    web backend:   install chrome-use (formerly agent-browser-stealth) and
                   connect its extension to your logged-in Chrome; be signed
                   in to chatgpt.com in that browser.
    codex backend: `npm i -g @openai/codex` and run `codex login`.

Project: https://github.com/leeguooooo/image-use
"""
